::meta
  title: Core PyPI Package Info
  version: 1.0.0
  domain: development|python|packages

::index
  # node-id    | type  | scope | conf | keywords
  core_pypi     | skill | all   | 1.0  | pypi, python package, pip package, python library, package version, wheel

::node[core_pypi]
->[:role=skill]
->[:keywords=pypi, python package, pip package, python library, package version, wheel]

::api[get_pypi_package]
method=GET
endpoint=https://pypi.org/pypi/{package}/json
schema={"package": "string"}
fields=info.name, info.version, info.summary, info.license, info.home_page, info.requires_python, info.requires_dist
description=Fetches the latest published release metadata of a Python package from the public PyPI registry: version, summary, license, homepage, Python version requirement, and dependencies. No API key needed.

::dense
PyPI package metadata: latest version, license, Python requirement, dependencies. No key needed.

::steps
1. Get the exact PyPI package name from the user (e.g., requests, fastapi, pydantic).
2. Call `get_pypi_package`.
3. Report `info.version`, `info.summary`, `info.license`, `info.home_page`, and `info.requires_python` if present. Summarise `info.requires_dist` briefly rather than listing every entry.
4. If the call returns an HTTP 404 error, tell the user the package was not found on PyPI and suggest checking the spelling.
5. Remind the user that this is the latest published release on PyPI, not necessarily the version installed in their environment (`uv pip show <package>` or `pip show <package>` shows that).

::assertions
assert package != "" "A PyPI package name is required."
